# FlyLab: A Dual-Scorecard Virtual Pharmacology Bench on the Drosophila MaleCNS Connectome

**Methods / software article, submitted draft for the International Journal of Research in Computing (IJRC)**

Software: https://github.com/pinkysworld/FlyLab
Version {{flylab_version}}, commit `{{git_sha}}`, compound library SHA-256 `{{library_sha256}}`, map `{{map_id}}`.

> Every numeric span in this document is generated by `scripts/reproduce_paper.py` from `papers/results.json`; the prose is written around the numbers, not the other way round. No number in this paper was measured in a living fly.

## Abstract

Virtual animals used in pharmacology teaching implement dose-response at the organ level and contain no neurons; public adult *Drosophila* connectomes and the point-neuron simulations built on them contain neurons and predicted transmitter signs but no ligand-receptor occupancy. FlyLab joins the two. A compound and a free concentration are scored as Hill occupancy on an insect receptor panel and on its vertebrate counterpart; the insect side alone is converted, through a fixed mechanism table, into gain multipliers on transmitter classes of a named MaleCNS v1.0 cut; a rate or leaky integrate-and-fire runtime produces named-cell readouts; and the run is exported as one notebook JSON with a provenance block. This article describes the software, the provenance discipline it enforces and the behaviour of the shipped model, including where it fails. At 1 µM, imidacloprid occupies the insect nAChR row at {{imi_insect_occ}} against {{imi_vert_occ}} at vertebrate α4β2, a {{imi_ec50_ratio}}-fold EC50 ratio. All {{lb_type_count}} labellar gustatory receptor-neuron types named by the recent gustatory nomenclature are present in MaleCNS v1.0 annotations, giving {{sweet_grn_cells}} sweet and {{bitter_grn_cells}} bitter seed cells and {{paths_sweet}} sweet and {{paths_bitter}} bitter GRN→MN9 paths of at most {{paths_max_len}} synapses. Driving those real cells reproduces a complete bitter veto of sugar-driven MN9 in vehicle (ratio {{veto_rate_vehicle_ratio}} on both engines), partially relieved by fipronil ({{veto_rate_fipronil_ratio}} rate, {{veto_lif_fipronil_ratio}} LIF). Against four connectome null models, the imidacloprid effect on the neighbourhood mean rate beats an Erdős–Rényi control decisively (z = {{null_z_imidacloprid_erdos_renyi}}) but does **not** beat degree-preserving or transmitter-histogram-preserving shuffles (max |z| = {{null_imi_max_structural_z}}), while fipronil does (z = {{null_z_fipronil_rewire_degree_preserving}} against degree-preserving rewiring): for a nicotinic agonist this readout is an excitation/inhibition-balance effect, not a wiring effect. A selectivity landscape over the library splits by mechanism rather than potency: sodium-channel and acetylcholinesterase compounds are amplified by the circuit, nicotinic compounds are buffered, by a mean selectivity-index gap of {{landscape_buffer_mean_gap}} log10 units. Retrospective rank validation against published orderings gives mean Spearman ρ = {{rank_mean_rho}} over {{rank_evaluated}} evaluable entries with {{rank_known_discrepancies}} recorded inversions. We claim a reproducible instrument and a set of pre-registered predictions, not a biological result.

**Index terms:** connectomics, *Drosophila melanogaster*, in-silico pharmacology, receptor occupancy, insecticide selectivity, null models, reproducible research, scientific software, 3Rs.

## 1. Introduction

Two bodies of software sit next to each other and do not touch.

The first is the virtual animal used in pharmacology teaching and procedural training. RatCVS [1], its web successors [2] and immersive variants such as PharmVR [3], [4] simulate an organ preparation: a dose goes in, a blood-pressure or contraction trace comes out. The parameters are pharmacological, the endpoints physiological, and the tools exist to reduce the animals used to teach a curve that is already known. What they lack is a nervous system — no named cells, no synapses, no way to ask which neuron a drug acted on.

The second is the connectome runtime. FlyWire's adult female brain [5] and the MaleCNS v1.0 release covering the male brain and ventral nerve cord [6] are public synapse-resolution wiring diagrams with a predicted transmitter per cell [10], [12]. A leaky integrate-and-fire model built directly on such a netlist recovers qualitative sensorimotor controls, including sugar-driven motor output and its suppression by bitter input [7]; the approach has been widely replicated and occasionally over-claimed [11]. What these runtimes lack is pharmacology. A connectome edge has a synapse count and a predicted transmitter; it has no EC50, no receptor subtype and no species counterpart.

The gap between them is the object of this paper. Pharmacology is a statement about receptors: imidacloprid is potent at insect nicotinic receptors and far weaker at many vertebrate subtypes, and that difference — not a difference in the nervous system as a whole — is the textbook explanation of its selective toxicity [8], [9], [35]–[37]. Connectomics is a statement about wiring. Neither answers the question a pharmacologist asks of a circuit: *at the concentration at which this compound occupies its insect target and not its vertebrate counterpart, what happens to a named cell?*

FlyLab's thesis is the **dual scorecard**: one compound and one free concentration produce two occupancy panels, insect and vertebrate, at the same dose, and only the insect panel may touch a circuit. The vertebrate panel is scored beside it and never runs a simulation, because no vertebrate connectome of comparable completeness exists and pretending otherwise would be the exact failure mode the project avoids. The circuit is a named cut of MaleCNS v1.0, chosen over FlyWire because MN9 and DNp01 must sit in the same atlas as the gustatory neurons that drive them, which requires the ventral nerve cord.

The contribution is threefold: an architecture in which the netlist and the pharmacological patch are separate, inspectable artefacts, so a reviewer can tell which number came from Janelia and which from a paper's EC50; a provenance discipline — evidence tiers on every receptor row, a refusal to invent a missing number, warnings that survive into the exported notebook — with evidence that it works, including one library error it caught; and an honest characterisation of the model's behaviour in which negative results are as prominent as positive ones, because the central circuit readout for nicotinic agonists turns out **not** to depend on the specific MaleCNS wiring.

We do not claim a new EM reconstruction, a fitted *in vivo* EC50, a replacement for regulatory toxicology, or any live-animal result. The `live_lab` field of every notebook this pipeline produced is `null`.

## 2. Related work

**Virtual animals without neurons.** RatCVS [1] models a pithed-rat cardiovascular preparation; Virtual Rat Web [2] ports the same pharmacology to a browser; PharmVR-style systems [3] add an immersive procedural layer, and the educational-evaluation literature [4] measures whether students learn from it. All are parameterised at the level of a tissue response. None contains a named neuron, so none can answer a circuit question, and none scores a second species at the same dose.

**Connectomes and connectome runtimes without pharmacology.** FlyWire [5] and MaleCNS v1.0 [6] publish the netlist and per-cell transmitter predictions [10], [12]. Shiu *et al.* [7] show that a literature-parameterised leaky integrate-and-fire model over that netlist reproduces sugar-driven motor output and the bitter veto without any fitting, which is the result FlyLab uses as a directional control. Downstream ports repeat the exercise with varying rigour [11]. In all of them a drug, if present at all, is a hand-set multiplier with no receptor behind it.

**Insect-versus-vertebrate receptor pharmacology.** The second scorecard rests on a mature literature: neonicotinoid selectivity at nicotinic receptors [8], [9], [35]–[37] and partial agonism at mammalian α7 [26]; phenylpyrazole and cyclodiene action at GABA-gated chloride channels [16], [27], [29], [31] with the β3-homopentamer caveat that undercuts naive "vertebrate-safe" framing [32]; avermectin selectivity for glutamate-gated chloride channels over glycine and GABA-A receptors [28], [33], [38]; and sodium-channel modulation with differential kdr sensitivity [23], [67]. It supplies teaching-order constants, not synapse-resolved occupancy — a distinction FlyLab enforces with an evidence tier on every row.

**Resistance alleles, expression atlases and combination pharmacology.** FlyLab also consumes three literatures that neither of the two traditions above uses computationally: published target-site resistance alleles as per-compound EC50 shifts [13]–[25]; single-cell expression atlases as a coarse, explicitly interpretive weighting of which cells can respond at all [39]–[44]; and the classical combination-effect null models [45], [46], [48], [49] with an empirical honeybee binary-mixture dataset as a validation target [51], alongside synergist data that show a sign reversal between susceptible and resistant strains [47], [50].

**Position.** No public tool writes one artefact holding an insect circuit response and a vertebrate receptor panel at the same dose with the provenance of every number attached. That artefact — the notebook — is what FlyLab is for.

## 3. Design goals and honesty rules

Five rules constrain the implementation and are enforced by tests.

1. **No invented constants.** Every receptor row in `flylab/pharm/library.yaml` carries a `source` and an `evidence_tier`. Of {{library_rows}} rows over {{library_compounds}} compounds, {{library_rows_sourced}} are `literature_order` and {{library_rows_placeholder}} are `class_placeholder` — EC50 0.01 M, `direction: none`, inert by construction rather than quietly plausible. A missing number is a reported absence, never filler.
2. **Two scorecards, one dose.** The vertebrate panel is computed at the same free concentration and never patches a circuit.
3. **The circuit fails loudly.** Every notebook carries a `warnings` list — hops-limited neighbourhood, predicted transmitter signs, teaching gain rules — in the exported artefact, not in a console message.
4. **At most one fitted parameter, and not yet.** v0.5 fits nothing to animal data; the only fits are of the *model's own* curves, labelled `model_derived`.
5. **No live-animal numbers.** `live_lab` stays `null` until a user imports a real table by hand; `protocols/` holds unexecuted drafts.

## 4. Methods

### 4.1 Occupancy and the sourced library

Fractional occupancy θ of a receptor at free concentration *C* is the Hill function

    θ(C) = C^n / (EC50^n + C^n)

with per-row EC50 and Hill coefficient *n*. The library declares {{library_receptor_classes}} receptor classes — insect nAChR, RDL, GluCl, AChE, Nav and OctR, and their vertebrate counterparts α4β2, α7, GABA-A, GlyR, AChE and Nav1.x — together with the insect/vertebrate pairs that define the scorecard. `compare_compound()` returns one row per declared receptor (occupancy, EC50, Hill *n*, direction, efficacy, source, evidence tier) plus a selectivity block per pair: the EC50 ratio, its base-10 logarithm, both occupancies, their difference, and a `placeholder` flag that is true whenever either side of the pair has no sourced number. Table T1 is the full library with tiers and sources, generated from the YAML.

Directions come from a fixed vocabulary (`agonist`, `partial_agonist`, `antagonist`, `positive_modulator`, `negative_modulator`, `inhibitor`, `none`), which matters because the mechanism table keys on direction, not on compound class.

### 4.2 Mechanism → gain rules

`flylab/pharm/mechanisms.py` is the single source of truth mapping occupancy rows onto circuit gains, and every assay obtains its gains from it. Gains are dimensionless multipliers on the synaptic weight of a transmitter class (or, for `g_nav`, on node excitability); 1.0 is vehicle and all gains are floored at 0.05, because a network with a gain of exactly zero is not informative. The {{mechanism_rules}} rules are listed in full in Table T2. The four that were shipped in v0.4 are reproduced verbatim and may not be changed silently:

* insect nAChR, activating: `g_ach = max(0.05, 1 + 0.4θ − 1.6θ²)`. The curve rises at low occupancy and falls at high occupancy, which encodes the classical two-phase nicotinic picture: excitation, then desensitisation and depolarisation block. At θ = 0.99 it saturates at the floor, which is why a high dose of a nicotinic agonist silences cholinergic transmission in the model rather than merely reducing it.
* insect nAChR, blocking: `g_ach = max(0.05, 1 − θ)`. A competitive blocker removes that fraction of cholinergic drive.
* insect RDL, blocking: `g_gaba = max(0.05, 1 − θ)`. Blocking the GABA-gated chloride channel removes inhibition.
* insect RDL, activating: `g_gaba = max(0.05, 1 + 0.4θ)`. Direct or allosteric opening adds inhibitory chloride current.

Version 0.5 adds four families with the same shape and a stated rationale each: GluCl activation uses a larger coefficient (`1 + 0.8θ`) than RDL because avermectin opening is effectively irreversible [38]; AChE inhibition raises an ambient cholinergic tone (`ach_tone = 1 + 2.0θ`) and multiplies `g_ach` by that tone and the agonist curve, reproducing the organophosphate excitation-then-block picture; Nav modulation scales node excitability globally (`g_nav = 1 + 1.5θ`), since pyrethroids and DDT act on every cell regardless of transmitter [23], [67]; octopamine agonism uses the smallest coefficient (`1 + 0.5θ`) because it is modulatory. These coefficients are modelling choices, not measurements, and §5.7 quantifies how dominant they are.

### 4.3 The two MaleCNS extracts

The public MaleCNS v1.0 flat connectome comprises a body-annotation table, a per-body neurotransmitter table and a ~1.1 GB weight matrix. None is committed. They are cut on a GitHub Actions runner by `flylab/maps/extract.py`, which streams the weight matrix with `pyarrow.ipc.open_file` in record batches — a full pandas read exhausts runner memory — and keeps only rows touching the seed set. Seeds resolve from the annotation `type` column by exact then prefix match, so `LB1a` also matches `LB1a_L`.

Two cuts are committed, both ≤ 1.1 MB of JSON:

* **`named`** — 1 hop around `MN9` and `DNp01` at a weight floor of {{named_min_weight}} synapses: {{named_nodes}} nodes, {{named_edges}} edges. Default graph for the rate assay, the null models and the selectivity landscape.
* **`taste_motor`** — 1 hop around `MN9`, `DNp01` and the labellar types `LB1a`–`LB1d`, `LB3b`/`LB3c`, plus the *induced closure*: every edge of at least {{taste_closure_min_weight}} synapses whose endpoints are both already in the node set. The closure turns a star of disjoint fans into a graph with paths through it, which is why this cut has {{taste_nodes}} nodes but {{taste_edges}} edges.

The committed census (`data/derived/malecns_census_v1.json`) records {{census_traced}} traced bodies with predicted transmitters — {{census_ach}} cholinergic, {{census_glu}} glutamatergic, {{census_gaba}} GABAergic — so the whole-CNS assay runs with no download. Growing a cut is deliberately constrained: past about 1 MB of JSON the weight floor is raised rather than hops added, because a 2-hop cut of a motor neuron in a 25-million-edge CNS is not a neighbourhood, it is most of the animal.

### 4.4 Rate runtime

`flylab/circuit/rate.py` holds a dense signed weight matrix `W_signed[j,i] = sign(nt_i) × synapses(i→j)`, where the sign is +1 for acetylcholine, −1 for GABA and glutamate (the insect glutamatergic sign convention at GluCl-bearing targets) and 0 for cells with no consensus prediction. A gain patch scales the columns by the presynaptic transmitter's gain. The state update is a clipped leaky iteration,

    r ← clip((1 − α)·r + α·g_nav·(d + W_eff·r), 0, r_max)

with α = 0.3, 80 steps, `r_max` = 300 Hz and *d* the per-node external drive in Hz. It is deterministic, and a single evaluation of the {{named_nodes}}-node graph costs about 50 ms with single-threaded BLAS, which is what makes the null models and the selectivity landscape affordable.

### 4.5 LIF runtime and its calibration

`flylab/circuit/lif.py` implements a current-based leaky integrate-and-fire network with the parameters used by Shiu *et al.* [7]: τ_m = 20 ms, V_rest = −52 mV, V_th = −45 mV, V_reset = −52 mV, refractory 2.2 ms, exponential synaptic current with τ_s = 5 ms, forward Euler at dt = 0.1 ms. A presynaptic spike on edge *i*→*j* injects `w_scale × synapses × sign(nt_i) × gain(nt_i)` millivolts of charge into *j*.

Two implementation choices need to be stated plainly because they are modelling decisions, not literature values.

**The synaptic scale is a one-time calibration scan, not a fit to animal data.** `w_scale` = 0.05 mV per synapse and the drive weight of 0.5 mV per event were chosen once, by scanning, so that vehicle MN9 cells fire in a plausible band under the standard 40 Hz seed drive and the rest of the cut is neither silent nor saturated. No fly recording was used, no error was minimised against data, and the values were not revisited per compound. Under the project's parameter policy this does not consume the one fitted parameter the project is allowed, because nothing was fitted *to* — but it is a tuned constant and should be read as one.

**A background Poisson drive is required, and it is a choice.** Excising a 1-hop neighbourhood removes every input from outside it, and in the `named` cut most cells are input-only with respect to the retained edges: without an external source they never spike, the drug cannot act on them, and the network degenerates into a handful of driven seeds. Every non-seed cell therefore receives an independent Poisson drive at 0.65 × the seed rate, standing in for the amputated inputs. This is the most consequential free choice in the LIF arm: it sets the operating point, it is why LIF and rate absolute rates differ by two orders of magnitude (Fig. F5), and a different fraction would change every LIF number here. It does not change the *direction* of any result, which is why the LIF arm appears only as a second engine for a contrast the rate engine also shows.

### 4.6 Ensembles, uncertainty and model dose-response

Library EC50s are order-of-magnitude quantities, so the honest error bar is on their logarithm. `flylab/pharm/uncertainty.py` perturbs each non-placeholder `log10(EC50)` by a normal draw with sd 0.3 (roughly a factor of two either way); placeholder rows are held fixed, because perturbing a number that does not exist manufactures confidence. `flylab/assays/ensemble.py` layers three replicate sources — the RNG seed (spiking only), the EC50 jitter and a jitter on the external drive — and attaches an `uncertainty` block to an otherwise unjittered notebook, so the headline numbers still match a plain single run. The same module builds the model's own concentration-response curve over a nine-point ladder, fits a four-parameter Hill function by Nelder–Mead in log-concentration space and bootstraps it by resampling replicates within each concentration. The result is explicitly the **model's circuit IC50** — where a simulated readout is halfway between its low- and high-dose plateaus — and is neither a receptor nor an animal IC50. A one-at-a-time sensitivity tornado probes five knobs at ×2 and ÷2: the insect EC50, the Hill coefficient, the external drive, the gain-rule coefficients and the graph's synapse-weight threshold.

### 4.7 Exposure, genotype and mixtures

**Exposure.** `flylab/pharm/exposure.py` maps a dose and route onto *C(t)* and hence θ(t) in one compartment. Its absorption and elimination constants are class placeholders chosen so the three routes differ in the direction the literature describes, and are fitted to no *Drosophila* ADME dataset, because that dataset does not exist: of the seven quantities such a model needs, `data/literature/fly_pharmacokinetics.yaml` records only metabolite identity as well established [52], [54], [55], [58]. Adult haemolymph volume, adult body mass, cuticular penetration rate and any *Drosophila* elimination half-life are `null`; what is recorded is 25 nL recoverable per adult by nanolitre sampling [57], 174 ± 81 nL in third-instar larvae [56], a water-partitioning measurement [53], an ethanol-clearance entry that could not supply a rate [65], and an imidacloprid half-life of 4.5–5 h in *honeybee* [58] — the only verified insect half-life for a library compound. Two structural warnings sit in that file and in every exposure profile: haemolymph is the lowest-radioactivity compartment for imidacloprid, so a one-compartment haemolymph model under-estimates CNS exposure [59], and a substantial metabolic route runs through the gut microbiome, so a single-enzyme clearance term is wrong for this compound [54].

**Genotype.** `flylab/pharm/genotype.py` applies published target-site resistance alleles as multiplicative EC50 shifts from `data/literature/resistance_alleles.yaml` [13]–[25], enforcing one rule: **a shift applies per compound, never per receptor.** Two published findings make the naive implementation wrong — *Rdl* A301S shifts GABA and ethiprole potency on the expressed planthopper receptor but leaves fipronil essentially untouched [15], and *para* M918T reduces deltamethrin and permethrin sensitivity roughly 100-fold while leaving DDT almost unchanged [22]. An allele/compound pair with no sourced number is left unchanged and listed under `unshifted`, never extrapolated; a shift derived from a whole-animal resistance ratio carries an extra warning that it overstates the target-site change.

**Mixtures.** `flylab/pharm/mixtures.py` implements Bliss independence [45] and Loewe additivity [48], [49] as explicit null models, following the response-surface framing of Greco *et al.* [46], and selects between them per pair: Loewe where the components share a FlyLab receptor key, Bliss where their targets are disjoint. Combined occupancy goes through the same mechanism table and the same circuit and is scored by mean absolute rate displacement relative to vehicle; verdicts are compared with the binary-mixture set of Zhu *et al.* [51].

### 4.8 The expression-weighted sensitivity layer

A gain patch applied uniformly assumes every cell expresses the receptor. `flylab/pharm/expression.py` relaxes that assumption using class-level expression statements from single-cell atlases [39]–[44], mapped by hand onto MaleCNS superclasses, and interpolates each node's gain between 1.0 (no receptor, so the drug cannot act there) and the full scalar gain.

This layer is a **sensitivity analysis and never the primary model**, for a reason the dataset itself records: none of the cited atlases uses MaleCNS superclass labels, so every mapping row is tagged `cross_atlas_inference` and most are `unknown`. Across the two cuts and the five insect receptor keys, only {{expr_coverage_overall}} of cells sit in a class with any published statement, and receptor expression in identified adult motor neurons is a confirmed gap, so MN9 — the paper's main readout — carries the `unknown` default. The effect is nonetheless real: weighting shifts MN9 by {{expr_mn9_delta_hz}} Hz relative to the uniform patch at 1 µM imidacloprid. We report it as a bound on what the uniform assumption is worth, not as an improvement.

### 4.9 Connectome null models

The central methodological question for any connectome simulation is whether the wiring matters. `flylab/analysis/nullmodels.py` measures the same drug effect on degraded copies of the same cut. Four degradations, in increasing order of destruction:

* `sign_permute` — the consensus transmitter labels are permuted across nodes. The transmitter histogram, and therefore the global excitation/inhibition balance, is preserved exactly; *which* cell is cholinergic is destroyed.
* `weight_permute` — synapse counts are permuted across edges. Topology and transmitters are kept; the pairing between wiring and strength is destroyed.
* `rewire_degree_preserving` — double-edge swaps, roughly 10 × |E| accepted swaps. Every node keeps its in-degree, out-degree and transmitter; the wiring pattern is destroyed.
* `erdos_renyi` — same node and edge counts, endpoints drawn uniformly, weights drawn from the empirical weight list. Only the size of the graph survives.

Every mode preserves the node set, the node order and the seed block — the same named cells are driven and read in the shuffled graph — and never creates self-loops or duplicate edges. The effect is always `treated − vehicle` with both arms on the *same* graph, so a shuffle can change it only through the wiring. The reported *p* is the empirical two-sided permutation *p*, (k+1)/(n+1), with a floor of 1/(n+1) at {{null_n_shuffles}} shuffles.

### 4.10 Selectivity indices

The receptor selectivity index of a compound is log10(EC50_vertebrate / EC50_insect) for its best non-placeholder pair: a property of two numbers in a YAML file. The circuit selectivity index asks whether the circuit effect arrives before vertebrate occupancy does. Let *C*_circuit be the lowest concentration at which the relative change in the simulated network readout reaches 50 % of its vehicle value, interpolated on log concentration, and *C*_vert the concentration at which the most potent vertebrate target reaches 20 % occupancy, in closed form from the Hill equation. The circuit index is log10(*C*_vert) − log10(*C*_circuit). Their difference, the **SI gap**, is positive when the neighbourhood amplifies what the receptor numbers promise and negative when it buffers them.

### 4.11 Retrospective rank validation

`flylab/validation/rank.py` compares the model's ordering with published orderings in `data/literature/published_rank_orders.yaml` using Spearman's ρ and Kendall's τ-b, implemented without SciPy. Entries the library cannot cover come back flagged `skipped` with the missing compounds listed, never silently dropped. A `KNOWN_DISCREPANCIES` block records inversions and explains them rather than fixing them, because fixing one would mean editing a receptor EC50 to match a whole-animal number, and those are different quantities.

### 4.12 Notebook provenance

Every assay returns a notebook (schema 0.3) whose `provenance` block records the FlyLab version, the git commit, the SHA-256 of the compound library, the map name and citation, the RNG seed and the platform string. The library hash is what makes the honesty rules auditable: if a number changes, the hash changes, and a notebook produced before the change cannot be confused with one produced after.

## 5. Results: what the software does

Everything in this section is a property of the software, the teaching library and one or two hops-limited MaleCNS cuts. Nothing in it is a measurement from a living fly. All values are regenerated by `scripts/reproduce_paper.py` and stored keyed in `papers/results.json`.

### 5.1 The dual scorecard (Fig. F2, Fig. F3, Table T1)

At 1 µM, imidacloprid occupies the insect nAChR row at {{imi_insect_occ}} and vertebrate α4β2 at {{imi_vert_occ}} — an occupancy gap of {{imi_occ_gap}} from EC50s of {{imi_insect_ec50}} and {{imi_vert_ec50}}, a {{imi_ec50_ratio}}-fold ratio or {{window_log10_imidacloprid}} log10 units of window. This is the canonical teaching contrast [8], [9], [35]–[37]; the model reproduces it because it was given those numbers, and the value of computing it here is that the *same* object then patches a circuit.

Fipronil occupies insect RDL at {{fip_insect_occ}} and native heteromeric vertebrate GABA-A at {{fip_vert_occ}}, a ratio of only {{fip_ec50_ratio}}-fold ({{window_log10_fipronil}} log10 units). That vertebrate occupancy is substantial, and it is the honest consequence of the library correction in §5.11: fipronil's "vertebrate-safe" framing does not survive at 1 µM in this library, and the paper does not make it. The β3-homopentamer caveat [32] makes it worse still and is recorded in the library's source string.

Diazepam is the insect-side negative control: vertebrate GABA-A at {{dia_vert_gabaa_occ}}, insect RDL at {{dia_insect_rdl_occ}} because that row is a `{{dia_insect_rdl_tier}}` — benzodiazepines are inactive at expressed *Drosophila* RDL over 0.1–100 µM [17], so the honest representation is an inert placeholder rather than a small number. Figure F3 shows the four-compound panel ({{panel4}}) spanning the four insect target families the mechanism table can patch.

### 5.2 Gate 1: the labellar GRN types are present in MaleCNS v1.0 (Table T0)

The open question carried by this project since v0.4 was whether the labellar gustatory receptor-neuron types named in the recent gustatory nomenclature actually appear in MaleCNS annotations, or whether the seed list was aspirational. It is answered: all {{lb_type_count}} `LB*` types are present in the `type` column of the committed census, covering {{lb_cell_count}} labellar cells out of {{census_gustatory}} traced gustatory neurons. The six types FlyLab drives are {{sweet_grn_breakdown}} on the sweet side ({{sweet_grn_cells}} cells) and {{bitter_grn_breakdown}} on the bitter side ({{bitter_grn_cells}} cells).

Two caveats travel with this result and are recorded in every notebook that uses it. The sweet/bitter assignment of the types is a working hypothesis from the published nomenclature, not a MaleCNS annotation; and several of these cells (notably the whole of LB1b) carry `unclear` as their predicted transmitter, so they contribute no sign to the model.

### 5.3 The map-extracted taste-motor path (Fig. F4, Table T7)

The `taste_motor` cut contains {{paths_sweet}} sweet and {{paths_bitter}} bitter paths of at most {{paths_max_len}} synapses from a labellar GRN to MN9. The strongest routes are short and stereotyped: LB3c through gnathal interneurons on the sweet side, LB1c through a glutamatergic gnathal relay on the bitter side. Composition matters for everything downstream: acetylcholine carries {{taste_motor_frac_ach_syn}} of synaptic weight and GABA {{taste_motor_frac_gaba_syn}} (E/I ratio {{taste_motor_ei_ratio_syn}}), against {{named_frac_ach_syn}} and {{named_frac_gaba_syn}} (ratio {{named_ei_ratio_syn}}) in the `named` cut.

### 5.4 The map-based bitter veto and its partial pharmacological relief (Fig. F5)

Driving the {{veto_n_sweet_grn}} real sweet GRN body IDs at {{veto_sugar_hz}} Hz and reading MN9 — never driving MN9 directly — gives a sugar-evoked MN9 rate of {{veto_rate_vehicle_sugar}} Hz on the rate engine and {{veto_lif_vehicle_sugar}} Hz on the LIF engine. Adding bitter drive on the {{veto_n_bitter_grn}} bitter GRNs takes MN9 to zero on both engines: a bitter veto ratio of {{veto_rate_vehicle_ratio}} on the rate engine and {{veto_lif_vehicle_ratio}} on the LIF engine. The direction matches the published behavioural and modelling result [7], [63] and the reduced five-unit circuit that FlyLab retains as its directional control.

Fipronil at 1 µM blocks RDL, raises the sugar-only rate to {{veto_rate_fipronil_sugar}} Hz (rate) and {{veto_lif_fipronil_sugar}} Hz (LIF) and **partially** relieves the veto, to {{veto_rate_fipronil_ratio}} and {{veto_lif_fipronil_ratio}} respectively. Relief is partial because the 0.05 gain floor leaves residual inhibition and because part of the veto runs through glutamatergic relays that RDL block does not touch.

Imidacloprid at 1 µM is not a veto ratio of zero: it silences MN9 altogether ({{veto_rate_imidacloprid_sugar}} Hz under sugar drive), so the ratio is **undefined**. The cause is the agonist rule at saturating occupancy (§4.2) — `g_ach` reaches its floor and removes the cholinergic GRN→MN9 drive itself — and it is why the taste-map arm of the null panel carries no imidacloprid z-score.

### 5.5 Null models: the central negative result (Fig. F6, Table T6)

At {{null_n_shuffles}} shuffles per mode, imidacloprid's effect on the network mean rate of the `named` cut is {{null_real_effect_imidacloprid}} Hz. Against the Erdős–Rényi null it is overwhelming (z = {{null_z_imidacloprid_erdos_renyi}}), which says only that the graph has the right size. Against the three structure-preserving nulls it is not significant at all — sign-permutation z = {{null_z_imidacloprid_sign_permute}}, weight-permutation z = {{null_z_imidacloprid_weight_permute}}, degree-preserving rewiring z = {{null_z_imidacloprid_rewire_degree_preserving}}, with a maximum absolute z of {{null_imi_max_structural_z}}. The connectome-information score for imidacloprid is {{null_score_imidacloprid}}.

This is the paper's most useful negative result. Because `sign_permute` preserves the transmitter histogram exactly, a small |z| there means the effect follows the cut's *global excitation/inhibition balance* rather than the identity of the cholinergic cells. An agonist that collapses `g_ach` to its floor removes a fixed fraction of excitatory weight wherever that weight sits, so the mean rate falls by nearly the same amount on any graph with the same transmitter histogram and degree sequence. For this compound and this readout the MaleCNS wiring does very little work, and a paper reporting only the Erdős–Rényi comparison would be claiming otherwise.

Fipronil differs: its effect of {{null_real_effect_fipronil}} Hz beats the topology nulls (weight-permutation z = {{null_z_fipronil_weight_permute}}, degree-preserving rewiring z = {{null_z_fipronil_rewire_degree_preserving}}; score {{null_score_fipronil}}). Disinhibition depends on where the inhibition is, and that is a wiring fact.

A second negative appears in the same panel: the fipronil bitter-veto ratio on the `taste_motor` cut is **not** distinguishable from its shuffled controls (max |z| = {{null_fip_taste_max_z}} across the four modes at {{null_n_shuffles_taste}} shuffles). The veto ratio is therefore a behaviour of this model, not evidence that the specific wiring carries the veto; a stronger test needs more shuffles and a readout less dominated by the drive.

### 5.6 The selectivity landscape (Fig. F7, Table T8)

Of {{landscape_rows}} compound-graph rows, {{landscape_scored}} carry both indices ({{landscape_compounds_scored}} compounds on two graphs). The split is by mechanism, not by potency:

* **amplify** ({{landscape_amplify_rows}} rows: {{landscape_amplify_compounds}}) — mean SI gap {{landscape_amplify_mean_gap}} log10 units: the sodium-channel modulators and the AChE inhibitor, whose gains scale excitability or ambient tone globally and so reach a 50 % network change below what the receptor ratio predicts.
* **buffer** ({{landscape_buffer_rows}} rows: {{landscape_buffer_compounds}}) — mean SI gap {{landscape_buffer_mean_gap}} log10 units: exactly the nicotinic set. The circuit gives back that much of the promised selectivity because the agonist curve rises before it falls — at low occupancy `g_ach` is slightly *above* 1, so a small occupancy barely moves the network and the threshold arrives late.

The separation between the groups is {{landscape_gap_split}} log10 units. The two graphs give nearly identical indices for every compound, which is reassuring for the index and unexciting for the connectome: what the index sees is the composition of the cut, not its wiring.

Eight compounds — {{landscape_unscored_compounds}} — receive no circuit index at all: their largest relative change in the mean rate never reaches the 50 % threshold on either cut. These are the RDL and GluCl blockers and the weak nicotinics. Disinhibition here is bounded by the GABA share of the cut and by the 0.05 gain floor, so a compound acting only by removing inhibition cannot produce an arbitrarily large mean-rate change — a structural limit of the readout, reported rather than worked around by lowering the threshold.

### 5.7 Model dose-response and where the model's uncertainty actually lives (Fig. F8)

The model's own concentration-response for imidacloprid on the `named` cut fits a four-parameter Hill function with r² = {{ic50_r2}}, slope {{ic50_slope}}, and a circuit IC50 of {{ic50_M}} (bootstrap 95 % CI {{ic50_lo_M}} to {{ic50_hi_M}}, {{ic50_n_boot}} resamples), falling from {{ic50_top_hz}} Hz to {{ic50_bottom_hz}} Hz. The circuit IC50 is {{ic50_vs_receptor_ec50}}× the receptor EC50 it was built from, which is the quantitative version of the buffering result in §5.6.

The sensitivity tornado at 1 µM is more instructive than the fit. The dominant parameter is {{tornado_top_param}} (span {{tornado_span_gain_coef}} Hz), then the external drive ({{tornado_span_drive_hz}} Hz) and the graph's weight threshold ({{tornado_span_weight_threshold}} Hz). The parameters with **zero** span are {{tornado_zero_span_params}}: occupancy is saturated at 1 µM, so halving or doubling the EC50 or the Hill coefficient changes nothing. Two consequences follow. The gain-rule coefficients — the least defensible numbers in the model — matter most, so any future fitted parameter should be one of them; and a single high dose carries no information about an EC50, which is why the receptor-level predictions in §6 need ladders rather than single doses.

### 5.8 Retrospective rank validation and two recorded inversions (Fig. F10, Table T4)

Across {{rank_entries}} published orderings and two model assays, {{rank_evaluated}} entry-assay combinations are evaluable and {{rank_skipped}} are skipped for want of library coverage. Mean Spearman ρ is {{rank_mean_rho}} ({{rank_mean_rho_occupancy}} for the occupancy assay, {{rank_mean_rho_subgraph}} for the circuit assay), with {{rank_exact_occupancy}} exact ordering matches on the occupancy side.

{{rank_known_discrepancies}} inversions are recorded rather than repaired ({{rank_discrepancy_ids}}). Nitenpyram sits near the top of the library's insect nAChR potency ordering but is the least potent neonicotinoid in *Drosophila* whole-animal bioassays — eight-fold weaker than clothianidin in larvae and twenty-five-fold weaker in adults [30] — largely because of poor persistence. Clothianidin and imidacloprid are ordered the opposite way in the library from both the acute bioassays and the chronic-lifespan ordering [30], [34], although the gap is within the uncertainty of either number. Both are receptor-versus-whole-animal discrepancies: the published quantity contains uptake, cuticular penetration, distribution, metabolism and excretion, and FlyLab models none of those. Editing the library to match would destroy the provenance chain for a cosmetic gain in ρ.

### 5.9 Mixtures (Table T5)

At {{mix_conc_M}} per component, the model reproduces {{mix_n_matching}} of {{mix_n_targets}} published binary-mixture verdicts from the honeybee dataset of Zhu *et al.* [51] ({{mix_species}}). Imidacloprid with an organophosphate and imidacloprid with a pyrethroid are additive under a Bliss null, as published; imidacloprid with clothianidin — two agonists at the same receptor key — shows no interaction, which is exactly what Loewe additivity predicts for two compounds acting at one target and is the sharpest of the three tests. Of the three partners, {{mix_n_substituted}} are not in the library and are represented by a same-class substitute; that is recorded per row and is a real weakness of the test rather than a detail.

### 5.10 Genotype specificity (Fig. F9, Table T9)

The panel covers {{n_genotypes}} published alleles. The result the module exists to demonstrate is the per-compound specificity. *Rdl* A301S shifts GABA potency {{geno_gaba_rdl_A301S_nlug_fold}}-fold but fipronil only {{geno_fipronil_rdl_A301S_nlug_fold}}-fold [15]; *para* M918T shifts deltamethrin {{geno_deltamethrin_para_M918T_superkdr_fold}}-fold and DDT {{geno_ddt_para_M918T_superkdr_fold}}-fold [22]. A genotype toggle that shifted a receptor uniformly would be wrong in both cases, and both facts came from the literature dataset rather than from the model.

One row is deliberately ugly. *Rdl* A301G in *D. simulans* carries a {{geno_fipronil_rdl_A302G_dsim_fold}}-fold shift derived from a whole-animal resistance ratio, which drives fipronil occupancy to {{geno_fipronil_rdl_A302G_dsim_occ}} and is almost certainly an overstatement of the target-site change [19]. The notebook says so.

### 5.11 The provenance discipline caught a library error

While compiling the literature datasets, a cross-check of `flylab/pharm/library.yaml` against the papers it cites found that fipronil's `vertebrate_GABA_A` row carried 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ M while citing Ratra and Casida [31], whose vertebrate average IC50 for fipronil is 1103 nM ≈ 1.1 × 10⁻⁶ M. The library contradicted its own citation by roughly nine-fold. The value is corrected in v0.5 and the source string records both the correction and the β3-homopentamer caveat [32].

The consequence is not cosmetic. The uncorrected library gave a fipronil insect/vertebrate ratio of 333-fold; the corrected one gives {{fip_ec50_ratio}}-fold, and the vertebrate occupancy at 1 µM is {{fip_vert_occ}} rather than a tenth of that. A claim of vertebrate safety that the v0.4 draft could have made in passing does not survive the correction. This is the case for the evidence-tier discipline stated as a result: a source string that is checkable is a source string that eventually gets checked.

## 6. Pre-registered predictions (Table T3)

The following {{pred_n_hypotheses}} hypotheses are written before any live experiment and are labelled `software_prediction`. Each row of Table T3 carries the predicted effect, a model-internal 95 % interval from {{pred_n_rep}} replicates, and a suggested sample size per group. The `live_result` field is `null` for every row and will stay `null` until a real table is imported by hand.

Two warnings attach to every row. The intervals are **model-internal** — teaching-EC50 jitter, drive jitter and the RNG seed — and contain no biological variance, because the model has none. The resulting standardised effect sizes are therefore absurdly large, so the power calculation caps d at 1.0, a conventionally "large" biological effect, before computing n; every suggested n here is {{pred_max_n}} per group and means "what a large effect would need", not a model-derived power calculation. Binomial figures assume independent flies, which vial-based protocols violate, so each n is a lower bound.

* **H1** (receptor occupancy). At 1 µM, imidacloprid insect nAChR occupancy ≥ 0.9 while vertebrate α4β2 stays ≤ 0.2. Predicted gap {{pred_H1_effect}}. This is checkable against published expressed-receptor work rather than against flies.
* **H2** (PER, `protocols/per.md`). The same dose lowers cholinergic drive in the MN9/DNp01 neighbourhood: predicted change in network mean rate {{pred_H2_effect}} Hz. Live counterpart: reduced proboscis extension to sucrose after oral imidacloprid. The control rate this needs — response to 100 mM sucrose with its variance — could not be sourced, because that concentration is normally used as a pre-screen in which only fully responding flies are kept; the nearest usable substitute is 40 % responders at 1 h food deprivation rising to 68 % at 2 h in 463 wild-type flies [61], which also shows that deprivation time is a larger nuisance variable than most effects FlyLab would try to detect.
* **H3** (climbing, `protocols/climbing.md`). Fipronil at 1 µM lowers `g_gaba` and disinhibits the neighbourhood: predicted change {{pred_H3_effect}} Hz, opposite in sign to H2. Live counterpart: negative-geotaxis impairment, the best-served endpoint available — a manual protocol with a large published effect [66], the RING method [64] and an automated variant that fixes *n* and the scoring window [60].
* **H4** (negative control). Diazepam occupies vertebrate GABA-A and not insect RDL in this library, so it moves no fly readout: predicted effect exactly {{pred_H4_effect}}. A non-zero live effect would falsify either the library row [17] or the assumption that the compound reaches the CNS unmetabolised.
* **H5** (within-animal control). Bitter input vetoes sugar-driven MN9 on the map-extracted path: predicted change {{pred_H5_effect}} Hz relative to sugar alone, matching the published direction [7], [63].
* **H6** (the novel prediction). Fipronil relieves the bitter veto on the map path: predicted rise in the veto ratio {{pred_H6_effect}}. This is the one row that is not a restatement of something already in the literature, and it is also the row §5.5 shows is not distinguishable from a shuffled graph — so a live confirmation would support the pharmacology and not the wiring.
* **H7** (selectivity negative control). Picrotoxin perturbs the fly neighbourhood while its receptor selectivity index is ~0: predicted change {{pred_H7_effect}} Hz. Any insect-selectivity claim the tool makes must fail for picrotoxin, and picrotoxin's lack of insect-over-vertebrate selectivity is independently attested [31].

Rows where the model's own output disagrees with the pre-registered direction: {{pred_direction_mismatches}}.

## 7. Limitations

The following are limitations of the instrument, not caveats about the conclusions; the conclusions are already limited to software behaviour.

1. **Transmitter identity is predicted, not measured.** Signs come from per-cell predictions [10], [12]; in the `named` cut {{named_frac_ach_syn}} of synaptic weight is cholinergic and {{named_frac_gaba_syn}} GABAergic under them, so a systematic classifier error would propagate to every gain patch. A non-trivial share of cells, including the whole of one labellar bitter type, carry `unclear` and contribute no sign.
2. **`insect_nAChR` is one key where the biology has several receptors.** Croset *et al.* [40] show α1, α5, α6 and α7 dominate the expressing population while α2–α4 are sparse, and β1 is expressed in more than twice as many cells as β2. Spinosad acts through α6 and the neonicotinoids and sulfoximines through β1-containing receptors [30], [62] — different, only partly overlapping cell sets the current schema cannot represent. This is a structural limitation of the library, not a wrong number, and it caps how much the selectivity landscape of §5.6 can mean.
3. **Hill occupancy is not kinetics.** The model has equilibrium occupancy at a stated free concentration; desensitisation, use-dependence, the loss of deltamethrin binding sites per channel under super-kdr [23] and the near-irreversibility of avermectin opening are represented at best as a coefficient.
4. **The EC50s are teaching-tier.** {{library_rows_sourced}} of {{library_rows}} rows are `literature_order`, the rest inert placeholders. Fipronil's insect RDL value sits mid-way through a ~1500-fold literature spread between house-fly membrane binding [27], [31] and *Drosophila* RDL in oocytes [29], and the RDL agonist EC50 for GABA differs ~13-fold between *Drosophila* and planthopper preparations [15], [29]. None is a measured constant for the cells being simulated.
5. **The neighbourhoods are hops-limited.** A 1-hop cut with a weight floor is a fraction of MN9's inputs; missing inhibition can flip the sign of any readout, and §5.6 shows the disinhibition readout is bounded by what survived the cut.
6. **There is no live data.** Every effect size here is model-internal; §6 contains predictions.
7. **Expression coverage is {{expr_coverage_overall}}, and motor neurons are the gap.** The §4.8 weighting covers only that fraction of cells, the mapping onto MaleCNS superclasses is an unvalidated cross-atlas inference, and no cited source reports RDL or nAChR expression for identified adult leg or labellar motor neurons — MN9's class.
8. **Whole-animal resistance ratios overstate target-site change.** Applying one as a receptor EC50 shift attributes penetration and metabolism to the receptor; every such row is flagged, and §5.10 shows the worst case.
9. **The LIF background drive is a modelling choice** (§4.5): it exists because excision removed the real inputs, it sets the LIF operating point, and every LIF rate here depends on it.
10. **The null models are the right question asked at small n.** {{null_n_shuffles}} shuffles give a *p*-floor of 1/({{null_n_shuffles}}+1), and the Erdős–Rényi z-scores are numerically enormous only because the null variance collapses; read them as "decisively different", not quantitatively.
11. **There is no fly pharmacokinetics.** The exposure model is a class placeholder end to end (§4.7). A free concentration at the receptor is an *input* to FlyLab, not something it can predict from a dose, and the literature does not currently supply the constants that would change that.
12. **`--fast` is not the paper.** A fast run uses fewer shuffles, a coarser ladder and fewer replicates, so its z-scores and intervals are noisier than those reported here, which come from a default run.

## 8. Reproducibility

The entire results section is regenerated by one command from committed data:

```bash
python -m pip install -e ".[dev,viz]"
python scripts/reproduce_paper.py          # full run, ~3.5 min
python scripts/reproduce_paper.py --fast   # reduced replicates, ~1.5 min
python scripts/reproduce_paper.py --only nulls --outdir /tmp/x
```

`--only` is incremental: a partial run keeps what the previous run in that directory produced for the steps it did not repeat, so `results.json` cannot be silently truncated.

The run needs no download and no 1.1 GB weight matrix: it reads `data/derived/` (two neighbourhood cuts, the census fallback, the gustatory seed list), `data/literature/` (six sourced YAML datasets) and `flylab/pharm/library.yaml`. It writes `papers/figures/*.png` (300 dpi, colour-blind-safe, with `figures/captions.md`), `papers/tables/T*.csv` and `.md`, and `papers/results.json` — every scalar quoted above with its value, rendered text, unit and producing step, plus a provenance block carrying the FlyLab version, git SHA, library SHA-256, map identifiers, graph sizes and seeds. This document is rendered from `papers/IJRC_FlyLab_draft.md.in` by substituting those keys, so a number cannot drift between code and prose. Every stochastic component (library jitter, drive jitter, LIF Poisson drive, graph shuffles, bootstrap resampling) is seeded from one `--seed`, so two runs at the same commit give identical values. `tests/test_reproduce.py` runs two cheap steps into a temporary directory, validates `results.json` against a schema and asserts that every key this draft references exists; the full suite runs in CI on every push.

**The bench also runs with nothing installed.** The same `flylab` wheel runs inside a browser tab on Pyodide and is published as a static site by `.github/workflows/pages.yml`; `docs/PAGES.md` documents the build. There is no second implementation: the browser reaches the package through `flylab.browser.bridge` and a workstation through `flylab.server`, and `tests/test_browser_bridge.py` asserts that the two transports return equal JSON — every readout to 1 × 10⁻⁹, every warning, every provenance field — for a representative call to each of the 34 routes, including all five assays, the ensemble, sensitivity, the circuit IC50, mixtures, genotypes, selectivity, the pre-registered predictions and a null-model panel. A notebook exported from the published page therefore carries the same `library.yaml` SHA-256 and the same numbers as one exported locally: the subgraph assay returns `mean_hz` identical to the last digit in both, in 278 ms in the browser against 36 ms natively, and a 200 ms LIF run takes 736 ms against 295 ms. First load is about 9 s with the runtime vendored (site 16.6 MB; 1.6 MB when Pyodide is fetched from a CDN). Two caveats belong with the claim: `git_sha` is `null` in a browser notebook, because there is no git in WebAssembly — the build's SHA lives in `manifest.json` instead — and the browser's spike panel defaults to a 200 ms window rather than 500 ms. A reviewer can therefore re-run the assays behind these figures by opening one link.

Data licences: MaleCNS v1.0 and FlyWire are released CC-BY 4.0 by their consortia [5], [6] and must be cited before this software. The derived JSON cuts in `data/derived/` inherit that licence and record it in their `citation` field. The literature datasets in `data/literature/` contain only values extracted from the cited publications, each with a DOI or PMID. FlyLab's own code is MIT.

Archival: a Zenodo deposit of the tagged release, the two derived cuts and `papers/results.json` will carry the DOI **[Zenodo DOI to be minted on acceptance]**, which should be cited alongside the connectome papers.

## 9. Conclusion

FlyLab is a virtual pharmacology bench in which a compound and a concentration become an insect occupancy panel, a vertebrate panel at the same dose, a gain patch on named cells of the public MaleCNS v1.0 connectome, a circuit readout and one notebook with its provenance attached. This article documents the architecture, the mechanism rules, the two connectome cuts, the two runtimes and their calibration, and the analysis layer — null models, selectivity indices, rank validation, mixtures and genotype shifts — that exists to test the instrument rather than advertise it.

The instrument works in the sense a methods article needs: reproducible from committed data in minutes, every quoted number traceable to a source with an evidence tier, and its own failures reported. Those failures are the most useful part. A nicotinic agonist's effect on the neighbourhood mean rate does not survive a degree-preserving shuffle of the very connectome the tool is built on; the map-extracted bitter veto is not distinguishable from its shuffled controls at the shuffle count we could afford; the model is exactly insensitive to the receptor EC50 at a saturating dose; and correcting one library row against its own citation removed a vertebrate-safety claim.

What is missing for a results paper is unchanged and short: one live *Drosophila* assay, at most one fitted parameter, and subunit-resolved receptor keys. Until then the seven hypotheses in §6 are predictions, and this is a description of a tool.

## Statements

**AI assistance.** This manuscript and the software it describes were drafted with AI assistance. All numbers in the results, tables and figures were produced by the reproduction pipeline from committed data and are keyed in `papers/results.json`; no value was written by hand into the prose. No data were generated, imputed or estimated by a language model.

**Animal research.** No animal experiments were performed. No live-animal data are reported. The `live_lab` field of every notebook produced by this pipeline is `null`. The protocols in `protocols/` are unexecuted drafts, included so that a power calculation can be done before any fly is touched.

**Data and code availability.** Code: https://github.com/pinkysworld/FlyLab (MIT). Connectome data: official MaleCNS v1.0 and FlyWire releases, CC-BY 4.0, downloaded and not redistributed; derived cuts are committed under `data/derived/` with their citation strings. Literature datasets: `data/literature/`, each value carrying a DOI or PMID.

**Competing interests.** None declared.

**Funding.** None.

